Before we start, one important point: I don't have a source-confirmed exact filename for the .gitignore lesson from the tracker itself, so I won't invent one.
Concept 1 — What is .gitignore?
A .gitignore file tells Git which files and folders it should not track in a repository.
For example, in a JavaScript/Playwright project, you generally don't want Git to track generated or local-only files such as:
node_modules/
playwright-report/
test-results/
.env

A .gitignore can contain patterns like:
node_modules/
.env
test-results/
playwright-report/

Why do we use it?
Imagine you run:
npm install

This creates:
node_modules/

You normally don't want thousands of dependency files to be committed to Git. Instead, your repository keeps package.json and the lock file, and another developer can run npm install.
Similarly, .env may contain environment-specific configuration or secrets, so it should generally not be committed.
Important rule
.gitignore affects untracked files.
If a file is already tracked by Git, simply adding it to .gitignore does not automatically stop tracking it.
That's an important interview point. 👀
